WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.337
A robot must find the shortest path across a warehouse grid, avoiding shelves. That grid is a graph, and breadth-first search finds the shortest route.

00:00:12.537 --> 00:00:13.465
Pause and think.

00:00:13.865 --> 00:00:17.546
Which search finds the fewest-steps path in an unweighted graph?

00:00:17.946 --> 00:00:25.260
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:33.809
Graphs model networks: servers and switches, build dependencies, road maps, and robot occupancy grids.

00:00:33.877 --> 00:00:41.006
New word: graph. A set of nodes connected by edges. Picture it like this: A map of cities and roads.

00:00:41.406 --> 00:00:50.067
New word: occupancy. How many GPU threads/warps are active versus the maximum. Picture it like this: How full the classrooms are.

00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:54.626
A graph is just dots and lines.

00:00:55.076 --> 00:00:59.244
The dots are nodes, like cities. The lines are edges, like roads.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:08.998
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:01:09.398 --> 00:01:19.777
New word: edge. A connection between two nodes in a graph; also "the edge" = servers close to users. Picture it like this: A road between two cities.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:28.876
Roads can be one way, which makes a directed graph, or carry a cost, like travel time, which makes a weighted graph.

00:01:30.226 --> 00:01:33.338
Breadth first search explores like ripples in a pond.

00:01:33.788 --> 00:01:37.921
Start at A. First visit every neighbor one step away: B and C.

00:01:38.371 --> 00:01:41.517
Then everything two steps away: D and E.

00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:48.353
Because it goes level by level, BFS finds the shortest path when every edge has the same cost.

00:01:48.366 --> 00:01:59.024
New word: B F S. Breadth-First Search: explore a graph level by level using a queue. Picture it like this: Ripples spreading out from a stone dropped in a pond.

00:02:01.574 --> 00:02:05.498
Depth first search is like exploring a maze with one hand on the wall.

00:02:05.948 --> 00:02:09.837
Go as deep as possible: A, then B, then D, then F.

00:02:10.287 --> 00:02:14.188
When you hit a dead end, back up and try the next unexplored path.

00:02:14.638 --> 00:02:21.093
DFS uses recursion or an explicit stack, and it is perfect for flood fill and cycle detection.

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:34.295
New word: D F S. Depth-First Search: explore a graph by going as deep as possible before backing up. Picture it like this: Exploring a maze by always following one wall until a dead end, then backing up.

00:02:34.695 --> 00:02:43.762
New word: stack. Last-in-first-out structure; also the memory area holding function calls. Picture it like this: A stack of plates.

00:02:44.162 --> 00:02:53.915
New word: recursion. A function that solves a problem by calling itself on smaller pieces. Picture it like this: Russian dolls: open one to find a smaller one.

00:02:56.465 --> 00:03:03.513
Topological sort orders tasks so every dependency comes first, like building drivers before the app that uses them.

00:03:03.615 --> 00:03:14.180
New word: topological sort. An ordering of tasks so every task comes after the tasks it depends on. Picture it like this: Getting dressed: socks before shoes.

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:20.067
Count how many arrows point into each node. Start with nodes that have zero.

00:03:20.517 --> 00:03:25.057
Each time you finish a node, remove its arrows. New zeros become ready.

00:03:25.507 --> 00:03:29.094
If nodes are left over and none are ready, there is a cycle.

00:03:30.444 --> 00:03:31.304
Quick guide.

00:03:31.754 --> 00:03:34.424
Unweighted shortest path: BFS.

00:03:34.874 --> 00:03:38.763
Weighted, non negative shortest path: Dijkstra with a heap.

00:03:38.796 --> 00:03:51.172
New word: heap. A tree-shaped structure that always gives you the smallest (or largest) item quickly; also the dynamic memory area. Picture it like this: A tournament where the champion is always on top.

00:03:52.822 --> 00:03:58.023
Connected groups or flood fill: DFS, BFS, or union find.

00:03:58.473 --> 00:04:01.573
Dependencies and ordering: topological sort.

00:04:02.023 --> 00:04:05.216
Always track visited nodes, or you will loop forever.

00:04:06.566 --> 00:04:07.669
Graphs in your world.

00:04:08.119 --> 00:04:11.962
A PCIe topology is a tree you walk with depth first search.

00:04:12.039 --> 00:04:23.080
New word: PCIe. PCI Express: the modern high-speed serial version of PCI. Picture it like this: A multi-lane motorway for expansion cards.

00:04:24.689 --> 00:04:27.638
Device driver probe order is a topological sort.

00:04:27.656 --> 00:04:40.206
New word: probe. The driver function the kernel calls when it finds a device that matches the driver. Picture it like this: The first handshake when a translator is introduced to the person they will translate for.

00:04:41.856 --> 00:04:46.720
A GPU cluster fabric is a weighted graph where shortest paths matter for collectives.

00:04:46.822 --> 00:05:00.336
New word: G P U. Graphics Processing Unit: a chip with thousands of small cores that do the same math on lots of data at once. Picture it like this: A stadium of students each solving one simple sum at the same time.

00:05:02.872 --> 00:05:08.608
Graphs look scary, but most graph problems are BFS or DFS with a visited set.

00:05:08.568 --> 00:05:09.822
Back to the question.

00:05:10.222 --> 00:05:13.902
Which search finds the fewest-steps path in an unweighted graph?

00:05:14.302 --> 00:05:17.971
Breadth-first search, because it explores level by level.

00:05:19.171 --> 00:05:20.042
Quick recap.

00:05:20.442 --> 00:05:22.079
Graphs are nodes and edges.

00:05:22.479 --> 00:05:25.544
BFS uses a queue and finds shortest hops.

00:05:25.944 --> 00:05:29.369
DFS uses a stack or recursion and explores deep.
